-- Backward through the concept layer for a whole profile: an authored evaluation profile -> the rule-model edits that
-- would make the forward pass write it. Binds $model (the rule model extracted from the packet), $p (the packet) and
-- $profile (the authored profile: APPLICABILITY, DECISION BOUNDARY, MET, NOT MET, UNCERTAIN). Replies with node edits —
-- change, add or remove; one node per distinct requirement, no size limit; a "setting" group holds where the rule is
-- meaningfully tested — each with the packet passage it rests on, or an empty source when the packet does not imply
-- it. Applying them is deterministic; the forward pass then re-derives every part from the edited model, so the
-- edited model is a structured account of what the authors require, and its empty-source nodes of what the packet
-- cannot imply.
-- The pass settles: read the same profile back from the edited model and it proposes little or nothing more (on the
-- invented spec 0.62 -> 0.25 -> 0 edits a profile; edit-loop.py --fixpoint). Read a maintained profile back from that
-- fixed point and the round trip of an edit is close to exact (edit-loop.py --roundtrip --anchor). Maintaining from the
-- settled model itself changes nothing for the edits (--anchor alone): settle for the check, not for the edit.
$json%($parse%(=>("You are computing a BACKWARD pass through a rule model. A program writes an evaluation profile from the RULE MODEL below: the setting says where the rule is meaningfully tested; MET holds when the action occurs, every condition holds and no permission applies; NOT MET is the exact complement; UNCERTAIN names facts that decide between them. The authors wrote the PROFILE below. Find the edits to the RULE MODEL that would make the program write what the profile requires, so the profile could be maintained as the model.\n\nRules:\n- Change the model, not the wording: requirements the model already states need no edit, however differently the profile words them.\n- One node per distinct requirement the profile makes; there is no limit on the number of nodes.\n- A testing-setting inclusion or exclusion is a \"setting\" node; an exception or permitted contrast is a permission; a requirement for MET is a condition; a narrowed or widened condition is an edit of that condition's text; a deciding unknown or calibration point is a fact; remove a node the profile contradicts.\n- Give each edited or added node the packet passage it rests on, quoted exactly from the PACKET, or an empty source if the packet does not imply it.\n\nReply with JSON only: {\"edits\": [{\"op\": \"edit\" | \"add\" | \"remove\", \"group\": \"action\" | \"setting\" | \"conditions\" | \"permissions\" | \"facts\", \"id\": \"<the node's id, for edit and remove>\", \"text\": \"<the node's new text, for edit and add>\", \"source\": \"<packet passage or empty>\"}], \"why\": \"<one sentence>\"}\n\nRULE MODEL:\n" ++ $json%$model ++ "\n\nPACKET:\n" ++ $json%$p ++ "\n\nPROFILE:\n" ++ $json%$profile)))
